# 8. DEPARTMENT MANAGEMENT

Implement departments.

Example:

```text
Engineering
QA
HR
Finance
Operations
Sales
Administration
```

Requirements:

* Unique department code within tenant
* Department name
* Description
* Status
* Branch/organization relationship where appropriate
* Audit fields
* Tenant isolation

Support future department hierarchy where practical.

---

# 9. DESIGNATION MANAGEMENT

Implement designation/job-title management.

Example:

```text
Software Engineer
Senior Software Engineer
QA Engineer
Automation Engineer
HR Manager
Finance Manager
Team Lead
Project Manager
```

Fields should support:

* id
* tenant_id
* designation_code
* designation_name
* description
* level/grade
* status
* audit fields

Design the model so future payroll and reporting modules can use designation levels.

---

# 10. EMPLOYEE MANAGEMENT

This is the most important component of Phase 3.

Create a production-ready employee module.

Employee should support information such as:

### Identity

* employee_id
* employee_code
* first_name
* middle_name
* last_name
* display_name
* gender where legally/business appropriate
* date_of_birth
* profile_photo reference

### Contact

* personal_email
* work_email
* personal_phone
* emergency contact reference

### Employment

* date_of_joining
* date_of_confirmation
* date_of_leaving
* employment_status
* employment_type
* employee_category
* department
* designation
* branch
* reporting_manager
* HR owner where applicable

### Work information

* employee_code
* work_email
* branch
* department
* designation
* manager
* shift assignment reference for future use

### Address

Support structured address information.

### Audit

Include:

* created_at
* updated_at
* created_by
* updated_by

Do not store unnecessary sensitive information.

---

# 11. EMPLOYEE STATUS

Create a controlled employee lifecycle.

Example statuses:

```text
ACTIVE
PROBATION
ON_NOTICE
ON_LEAVE
SUSPENDED
INACTIVE
TERMINATED
RESIGNED
RETIRED
```

Use an enum or controlled reference system consistent with the existing architecture.

Do not allow arbitrary status strings.

---

# 12. EMPLOYMENT TYPE

Support:

```text
FULL_TIME
PART_TIME
CONTRACT
INTERN
CONSULTANT
TEMPORARY
```

Keep this extensible.

---

# 13. EMPLOYEE ↔ USER ACCOUNT

An employee and a user account must NOT automatically be treated as the same entity.

Design the relationship properly.

Example:

```text
Employee
   │
   └── optional User Account
```

Possible scenarios:

### Employee with login

```text
Employee
   ↓
User
   ↓
Role
```

### Employee without login

```text
Employee
   ↓
No User Account
```

The system must support both.

Prevent accidental duplicate accounts.

---

# 14. REPORTING HIERARCHY

Implement employee-manager relationships.

Example:

```text
CEO
 ├── HR Manager
 │    ├── HR Executive
 │    └── Recruiter
 │
 └── Engineering Manager
      ├── Team Lead
      │    ├── Developer
      │    └── QA
      └── Team Lead
```

Requirements:

* Employee can have a reporting manager.
* Manager must belong to the same tenant.
* Employee cannot report to themselves.
* Prevent invalid hierarchy cycles.
* Support future approval workflows.

---

# 15. EMPLOYEE DOCUMENT ARCHITECTURE

Prepare an employee document module.

Do NOT unnecessarily store large binary files directly in PostgreSQL.

Create metadata architecture supporting:

* document_id
* employee_id
* document_type
* file_name
* storage_key/reference
* mime_type
* file_size
* uploaded_by
* uploaded_at
* verification_status
* expiry_date where applicable

Future storage may use:

* S3
* Azure Blob
* Google Cloud Storage
* local development storage

The database should primarily store metadata/storage references.

---

# 16. PROFILE PHOTO ARCHITECTURE

Prepare employee profile photo support.

Do not implement biometric face recognition in Phase 3.

Only establish:

* photo metadata
* secure storage reference
* upload/update/delete architecture
* authorization
* tenant isolation

Future attendance/selfie verification will extend this.

